2.text:
PRIVATE REFLECTION: The five positives are all CVC and the five negatives are all ruled out by shape/position.

* Positives dad dab gut low cat are C1 V2 C3 with V2∈{a,u,o} and C3∈{b,d,t,w}
  dad d a d  V=a C3=d
  dab d a b  V=a C3=b
  gut g u t  V=u C3=t
  low l o w  V=o C3=w
  cat c a t  V=a C3=t

The quoted negatives are ruled out by the same test
ear e a r   C1 not C, C3=r∉{b,d,t,w}
cop c o p   V=o ok but C3=p∉{b,d,t,w}
ego e g o   V2=g∉{a,u,o}
mom m o m   V=o ok but C3=m∉{b,d,t,w}
ate a t e   V2=t∉{a,u,o}

That gives a very tight V2/C3 characterisation. Under it

leg l e g   V2=e∉{a,u,o}, C3=g∉{b,d,t,w}
dam d a m   V2=a ok but C3=m∉{b,d,t,w}
rat r a t   V2=a ok, C3=t∈{b,d,t,w}  ← the only list item that satisfies both
car c a r   V2=a ok but C3=r∉{b,d,t,w}
bin b i n   V2=i∉{a,u,o}, C3=n∉{b,d,t,w}

So rat is the only option of the same CVC V2∈{a,u,o} C3∈{b,d,t,w} type as the five exemplars. A,B,D,E are all non-members. Leg is the canonical non-member on both vowel and coda grounds.

That is in tension with the looser “no e/m/p/r” / “no m/p coda” family of hypotheses that colleagues 1,2,4,5,6 raise. Those hypotheses make e/m/p/r avoidance the discriminator and would make bin the only e/m/p/r-free item, with leg/dam/rat/car all bad, and some versions make dam the unique m/p-coda violator. The V2/C3 inventory above is the strictest description that accounts for ego/ate shape failures and for the i in bin. The discussion so far converges on rat being the unique good list item, i.e. A,B,D,E are valid non-followers, with leg, dam, car, bin all clearly violating the V2/C3 inventory. Unresolved is whether the intended problem wants the single “most obvious” non-follower – leg – or acknowledges the four-way non-membership.

6.text:
PRIVATE REFLECTION: The five positives are all three-letter CVCs.

dad dab gut low cat
* vowel set = {a,u,o}
* C3 set = {b,d,t,w}
* letter inventory = {a,b,c,d,g,l,o,t,u,w}
No positive uses e,m,r,p.

The five quoted negatives are all ruled out by the same inventory.

ear – e,r
cop – p, CVC with bad coda
ego – VCV, e
mom – m, CVC with bad coda
ate – VCV, e
Every negative contains ≥1 of {e,m,r,p} and the CVC negatives cop,mom are distinguished from the positives by coda.

That gives at least two coherent descriptions that fit the exemplars:

* Uniform letter-absence: CVC and e,m,r,p-free. Then good = {a,b,c,d,g,l,o,t,u,w}, bad = contains e/m/r/p.
* CVC + V2∈{a,u,o} + C3∈{b,d,t,w}. That is equivalent to the letter-absence description for the given items, and makes the CVC negatives cop,mom fail on coda p,m and ear fail on coda r.

On the options, which are all CVC:

A leg = l e g – e in it, V2=e, C3=g∉{b,d,t,w}
B dam = d a m – m in it, C3=m∉{b,d,t,w}
C rat = r a t – r in it, V2=a, C3=t∈{b,d,t,w}
D car = c a r – r in it, C3=r∉{b,d,t,w}
E bin = b i n – e,m,r,p-free, V2=i∉{a,u,o}, C3=n∉{b,d,t,w}

Under the strict letter-absence / {a,u,o}/{b,d,t,w} description the only option that is of the dad/dab/gut/low/cat type is C rat, and A leg, B dam, D car, E bin are all non-members. In the e/m/r/p-free reading the only option that is of the type is E bin, and A leg, B dam, C rat, D car are all non-members.

The disagreement in the round is therefore between the coda-only / m/p-only reading, which makes B dam the unique violator, the final-set reading which makes C rat the unique member and A leg/B dam/D car/E bin non-members, and the uniform letter-absence reading which makes E bin the unique member and A–D non-members. Leg is the canonical clear non-member under all readings: it introduces the forbidden vowel e and a forbidden final g, and it is the first counter-example colleagues cite. The exact single-choice answer depends on which of the three equivalent-seeming restrictions – e/m/r/p-free, V2∈{a,u,o}+C3∈{b,d,t,w}, or CVC+ C3∉{m,p} – is taken as the intended minimal pattern; the evidence for a uniform forbidden-letter set is the most parsimonious across both positive and negative exemplars.

